  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn area of low pressure is currently located over Pennsylvania\nand will continue to track eastward off the mid-Atlantic coast \ntonight, then slowly east to northeastward away from the coast \non Friday. Widespread precipitation continues to fall across the\narea which is primarily rain, though snow is falling at \nelevations mainly above 1500 feet across the Adirondacks and \nsouthern Greens where some light snow accumulations have already\noccurred. Temperatures as of the 1pm hour range from the lower\nto mid-30s across much of the higher elevations to the upper 30s\nto mid-40s in the valleys. Temperatures have likely reached\ntheir maximum value today with a slow falling of temperatures\nexpected for the rest of this afternoon through tonight. As \ntemperatures fall tonight, precipitation will begin to mix with \nand/or change to all wet snow tonight with snow levels lowering \ntoward the valley areas toward daybreak. Elevations above 1000\nfeet should change to all snow with valley areas below 1000 feet\nhaving either all snow or a rain/snow mix. Latest guidance\nsuggests any mesoscale banding potential favors the eastern\nCatskills including in Schoharie County where 2 to 5 inches of\nsnow is expected, though some higher totals cannot be ruled out\nwhere the greatest banding occurs. With the increased amounts,\nthe Winter Weather Advisory was expanded into Schoharie County.\nFor the Mohawk and Hudson Valley areas, expect a transition to a\nrain/snow mix or all snow for a brief time tonight into Friday\nmorning where a light coating of accumulation will be possible,\nthough likely only accumulating on grassy surfaces.\n\nAs the low pulls away on Friday, precipitation will become light\nand be either snow or a rain/snow mix but gradually taper off\nfrom north to south throughout the day. The best additional\nsnowfall accumulations will be across the eastern Catskills\nwhere 1-2 inches of the storm total will likely occur after 7 am\nFriday morning. Elsewhere, little or no additional accumulation\nis expected. It will remain cloudy and chilly on Friday with \ntemperatures only in the 30s to lower 40s. Temperatures fall \nback below freezing Friday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAn upper level trough will linger across the area this weekend.\nMoist, upslope flow and northwesterly flow off the Great Lakes\nwill bring additional light rain and snow showers this weekend\nwith the greatest precipitation chances for areas outside of the\nHudson Valley. However, a isolated shower or flurry cannot be\nruled out for parts of the Hudson Valley. Additional coatings to\nlocally 1 inch of accumulation will be possible this weekend\nmainly for the Adirondacks into the southern Greens.\n\nUpper level ridging and surface high pressure builds into the \nregion for next week bringing a trend of largely dry weather. \nTemperatures will slowly trend upward each day rising to above \nnormal levels by mid to late next week.",
